<h2>Estimated band conditions</h2>

{% if not m.bands.unavailable and m.bands.hf %}
<p>A rough guide to the high-frequency bands, day and night, estimated from
the solar flux and Kp above — <strong>not a measurement</strong>. Each rating
is shown three ways: the word, a symbol (filled = better), and a color. For
what's actually open right now, real-time reports beat any estimate:
<a href="https://prop.kc2g.com/">prop.kc2g.com</a> and
<a href="https://pskreporter.info/">PSK Reporter</a>.</p>

<table class="bands">
  <caption>Estimated HF conditions. ● Good, ◐ Fair, ○ Poor, · Closed.</caption>
  <thead>
    <tr><th scope="col">Band</th><th scope="col">Day</th><th scope="col">Night</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {% for b in m.bands.hf %}
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">{{ b.band }}</th>
      <td class="cond cond-{{ b.day.cls }}"><span class="cond-mark" aria-hidden="true">{{ b.day.mark }}</span> {{ b.day.label }}</td>
      <td class="cond cond-{{ b.night.cls }}"><span class="cond-mark" aria-hidden="true">{{ b.night.mark }}</span> {{ b.night.label }}</td>
    </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>

<p class="vhf-note"><strong>VHF:</strong> the higher bands don't follow solar
flux — they open on sporadic-E and aurora. <strong>6 m</strong> — {{ m.bands.vhf.six_m }}.
<strong>2 m</strong> — {{ m.bands.vhf.two_m }}.</p>
{% else %}
<p>Band-condition estimates need both the solar flux and Kp; one of them is
unavailable right now.</p>
{% endif %}

<h2>Radio blackout, radiation, and geomagnetic scales</h2>

<p>NOAA rates three kinds of space weather on a 1-to-5 scale; level 0 means
none. The scale names are NOAA's; the short radio notes are added here.</p>

{% if not m.scales_now.unavailable %}
<ul class="scales">
  <li><strong class="badge badge-{{ m.scales_now.R.scale | sevClass }}">R{{ m.scales_now.R.scale or "0" }}</strong>
    radio blackouts —
    {% if (m.scales_now.R.scale or "0") == "0" %}none; HF is not being degraded by flares.{% else %}{{ m.scales_now.R.text }}: HF radio degraded on the sunlit side.{% endif %}</li>
  <li><strong class="badge badge-{{ m.scales_now.S.scale | sevClass }}">S{{ m.scales_now.S.scale or "0" }}</strong>
    solar radiation storms —
    {% if (m.scales_now.S.scale or "0") == "0" %}none.{% else %}{{ m.scales_now.S.text }}: raised absorption near the poles.{% endif %}</li>
  <li><strong class="badge badge-{{ m.scales_now.G.scale | sevClass }}">G{{ m.scales_now.G.scale or "0" }}</strong>
    geomagnetic storms —
    {% if (m.scales_now.G.scale or "0") == "0" %}none; the geomagnetic field is stable.{% else %}{{ m.scales_now.G.text }}: HF fades at higher latitudes, aurora more likely.{% endif %}</li>
</ul>
{% else %}
<p>Scale data is unavailable right now.</p>
{% endif %}

<h2>Geomagnetic detail</h2>

{% if not m.kp.unavailable %}
<p>{{ m.kp.words }}.</p>
<p class="kp-meter-row">Kp scale 0 to 9:
  <span class="kp-meter kp-{{ m.kp.cls }}" aria-hidden="true"><span class="kp-filled">{{ "▮" | repeatChar(m.kp.cells) }}</span>{{ "▯" | repeatChar(9 - m.kp.cells) }}</span>
  <span class="kp-meter-text">({{ m.kp.cells }} of 9)</span></p>
{% endif %}
<p>NOAA's storm scale notes how far south aurora has been seen: at Kp 7
(G3), as far as Oregon and Illinois — around 50° geomagnetic latitude; at
Kp 6 (G2), New York and Idaho. Below that it stays well to the north.</p>
{% if not m.aurora_power.unavailable %}
<p>Aurora power now: {{ m.aurora_power.words }}.{{ note(m.aurora_power) }}
  (Higher power means a brighter, lower-reaching oval.)</p>
{% endif %}

