<h2 id="reading">Reading this page</h2>

<p>None of the information here depends on color — every reading is written
out in words, so it works the same in a screen reader, a text browser, or
on paper. Here is what the numbers mean.</p>

<dl class="glossary">
  <dt><dfn>SFI, the solar flux index</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A measure of the Sun's radio output at 10.7 cm, which tracks how well
    the upper ionosphere reflects signals. Higher generally means the higher
    HF bands (10, 12, 15 metres) open up. The band interpretations here follow
    Paul Herrman, N0NBH (<a href="https://www.hamqsl.com/solar2.html">hamqsl.com</a>);
    NOAA publishes the raw number, not the band verdicts.</dd>

  <dt><dfn>Sunspot number</dfn></dt>
  <dd>A daily count of sunspots and sunspot groups. It rises and falls with
    the roughly 11-year solar cycle and moves with the solar flux.</dd>

  <dt><dfn>Kp, the planetary K-index</dfn></dt>
  <dd>How disturbed Earth's magnetic field is, from 0 (calm) to 9 (extreme
    storm), averaged over three hours from magnetometers worldwide. Higher
    Kp means more high-latitude HF trouble and a better chance of aurora.
    NOAA counts Kp 5 and above as a geomagnetic storm.</dd>

  <dt><dfn>Solar wind and Bz</dfn></dt>
  <dd>The stream of particles from the Sun, and the north–south tilt of the
    magnetic field it carries (Bz). A southward Bz, roughly −5 nanotesla or
    lower, lets the solar wind pour energy into Earth's field and drive
    storms; a northward Bz keeps things quiet.</dd>

  <dt><dfn>WWV</dfn></dt>
  <dd>The time-standard station whose hourly voice bulletins have carried
    these numbers to operators for decades. NOAA's text version is quoted
    below, unchanged.</dd>
</dl>

<p>Times on this page are in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC), the standard
for space-weather data — the same clock the net's log and most radio
operation use.</p>

{% if not m.wwv.unavailable and m.wwv.text %}
<h2>Latest WWV bulletin</h2>
